1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:08,353
In 1925, a man walked into a luxury hotel in Paris, and claimed he had the authority to sell the Eiffel Tower.

2
00:00:08,303 --> 00:00:10,867
His name was Victor Lustig.

3
00:00:10,867 --> 00:00:14,005
And his plan was ridiculously simple.

4
00:00:14,005 --> 00:00:21,913
He forged government documents, posed as a French official, and secretly invited several wealthy scrap metal dealers to a meeting.

5
00:00:21,913 --> 00:00:29,821
He told them the Eiffel Tower was becoming too expensive to maintain, and the government had decided to tear it down and sell the metal.

6
00:00:29,821 --> 00:00:34,834
Then Lustig chose his target: a businessman named André Poisson.

7
00:00:34,834 --> 00:00:37,780
But Lustig didn't just ask for money.

8
00:00:37,780 --> 00:00:41,619
He hinted that he was a corrupt official who needed a bribe.

9
00:00:41,619 --> 00:00:45,127
And somehow, that made him look more legitimate.

10
00:00:45,127 --> 00:00:47,589
Poisson paid him a fortune.

11
00:00:47,589 --> 00:00:50,344
Lustig disappeared to Austria.

12
00:00:50,344 --> 00:00:55,535
And when the newspapers stayed silent, Lustig realized something incredible.

13
00:00:55,535 --> 00:01:00,165
He came back to Paris, and tried to sell the Eiffel Tower again.

